e that's coterminal with $\\frac{11\\pi }{6}$ that lies within the range of $\\theta ,$ we subtract $2\\pi .$\n\n$$\n\\frac{11\\pi }{6}-2\\pi =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{1}{2})=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295402 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e89b620-641a-5dbf-85f6-ca0f7a2fad80" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Arcsine or Arccosine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "73451c54-cbbc-5c37-9be5-5642b43b833c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295404}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "73451c54-cbbc-5c37-9be5-5642b43b833c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295404} :answer-field/id #uuid "c099d0f2-dc67-5192-a1ea-a01fa8bb33c7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295403}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "81621355-aec4-54de-931c-fbe290ebf284" :question/math-academy-id "q-320667" :question/problem "Expressed in degrees, $\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})=${{field-1}}${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-{90}^{∘}\\le \\theta \\le {90}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta $ spans the first and fourth quadrants.\n\nSince $\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ is positive, our answer is an angle that lies in the first quadrant, where sine is positive.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14035417/assets/d93c490adbb5cdbc0e9a09b60eea9b82ab93be8e871e334423abc17ddd62955e.png)\n\nFrom the values in the first quadrant, we see that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡({60}^{∘})=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})={60}^{∘}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278583 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3878b5fa-fd5e-5984-8516-96ee17bf137b" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Arctangent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8fea6824-130d-568a-8b22-651d4b7f4508" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278585} {:answer/id #uuid "10775f6f-2f96-538b-be2a-f768194d8350" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295405}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8fea6824-130d-568a-8b22-651d4b7f4508" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278585} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6baa3ed-3895-59e1-a671-f7ca80a57681" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278584}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f53bc9c3-fd24-580b-be8a-fd85b0a8922e" :question/math-academy-id "q-320730" :question/problem "Expressed in degrees, $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})=${{field-1}}${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-{90}^{∘}<\\theta <{90}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta $ spans the first and fourth quadrants.\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}$ is negative, our answer is an angle that lies in the fourth quadrant, where the tangent is negative.\n\nFrom the special values of the trigonometric functions and the oddness of the tangent function, we know that \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡(-{30}^{∘})=-\\operatorname{tan}⁡({30}^{∘})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\n$$\n\nSince $-{30}^{∘}$ is an angle in the fourth quadrant that lies within the range of $arctan,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})={-30}^{∘}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295406 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3878b5fa-fd5e-5984-8516-96ee17bf137b" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Arctangent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "494974ea-a870-5f9c-8113-051d52319093" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295408} {:answer/id #uuid "887e9a34-79f9-5914-b83b-54a4690ecf33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295409}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "494974ea-a870-5f9c-8113-051d52319093" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295408} :answer-field/id #uuid "99781a69-a70d-58eb-bf8f-ef7ff49d1c34" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295407}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dcd819ac-f54d-580d-9c30-ef00b37e0ddd" :question/math-academy-id "q-320789" :question/problem "Expressed in radians, $\\frac{1}{2}arctan\\,(\\sqrt{3})=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<\\theta <\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta $ spans the first and fourth quadrants.\n\nSince $\\sqrt{3}$ is positive, our answer is an angle that lies in the first quadrant, where the tangent is positive.\n\nFrom the special values of the trigonometric functions, we know that \n\n$$\ntan\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{3})=\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ is an angle in the first quadrant that lies within the range of $arctan,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\narctan\\,(\\sqrt{3})=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}arctan\\,(\\sqrt{3})=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295410 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7c2791d4-f801-5555-a859-1d373f91a67e" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Arcsine When a Coterminal Angle is Required"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0defb882-5142-5422-aa53-5788500c0972" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "240" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295412} {:answer/id #uuid "fb8896b0-fdf1-53b2-9cfc-fd3715665156" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-240" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295413}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb8896b0-fdf1-53b2-9cfc-fd3715665156" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-240" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295413} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf363c20-7a12-5225-abb8-7160a28e988e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295411}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "92ff9de5-848d-514f-9cce-7a8114e6d927" :question/math-academy-id "q-320888" :question/problem "Expressed in degrees, $4\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})=${{field-1}}${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-{90}^{∘}\\le \\theta \\le {90}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's take a look at the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14035417/assets/ca39c4cde40b99fd40a11746a31398d82814268b2df706e3101ee88648c9e2d4.png)\n\nFrom the values on the unit circle, we see that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡({300}^{∘})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nHowever, ${300}^{∘}$ does not lie within the range of $\\theta .$ To get an angle that's coterminal with ${300}^{∘}$ that lies within the range of $\\theta ,$ we subtract ${360}^{∘}.$\n\n$$\n{300}^{∘}-{360}^{∘}=-{60}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})=-{60}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) & =4\\cdot (-{60}^{∘}) \\\\  & ={-240}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295414 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7c2791d4-f801-5555-a859-1d373f91a67e" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Arcsine When a Coterminal Angle is Required"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6b25d3f3-a8df-57ad-b278-9ef793b8ed2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295416} {:answer/id #uuid "3331941a-dacd-5ae1-aaa3-380deb86c652" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295417}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6b25d3f3-a8df-57ad-b278-9ef793b8ed2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295416} :answer-field/id #uuid "c903b6ff-51f9-5ce4-99c3-cde7cc748304" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295415}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "79e652eb-fae0-5230-8687-a3f5dddc2056" :question/math-academy-id "q-321100" :question/problem "Expressed in radians, $arcsin\\,(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, note that the range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's take a look at the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14035417/assets/a33ea7c6d1aa7cd7e54376aefb2af4347da59a61ecaabdbf4964d41b9661870a.png)\n\nFrom the values in the unit circle, we see that\n\n$$\nsin\\,(\\frac{7\\pi }{4})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nHowever, $\\frac{7\\pi }{4}$ does not lie within the range of $\\theta .$ To get an angle that's coterminal with $\\frac{7\\pi }{4}$ that lies within the range of $\\theta ,$ we subtract $2\\pi .$\n\n$$\n\\frac{7\\pi }{4}-2\\pi =-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\narcsin\\,(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269035 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e89b620-641a-5dbf-85f6-ca0f7a2fad80" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Arcsine or Arccosine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b309955-8c8e-5971-a5aa-cd54076e91db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269037}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b309955-8c8e-5971-a5aa-cd54076e91db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269037} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6e5032b-544f-5a0f-aeb5-8c72adc02d4a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269036}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "78407dd9-1b78-5e45-bc8d-90e26337e6f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-321269" :question/problem "Expressed in degrees, $arccos\\,(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=${{field-1}}${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n{0}^{∘}\\le \\theta \\le {180}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta $ spans the first and second quadrants.\n\nSince $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ is positive, our answer is an angle that lies in the first quadrant, where cosine is positive.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14035417/assets/ca39c4cde40b99fd40a11746a31398d82814268b2df706e3101ee88648c9e2d4.png)\n\nFrom the values in the first quadrant, we see that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡({45}^{∘})=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\narccos\\,(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})={45}^{∘}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261854 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7c2791d4-f801-5555-a859-1d373f91a67e" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Arcsine When a Coterminal Angle is Required"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ec5c2c8-2dca-5dbc-aaad-7020b7e4921f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{60}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295419} {:answer/id #uuid "0b1b7a55-6483-504b-8ec1-fd46abab4a14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{45}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295420} {:answer/id #uuid "4403927e-ec8a-5e84-8297-b2b322b4e47e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{30}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295421} {:answer/id #uuid "4e7af426-8d29-5c6f-bd53-1e2d4f62d650" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{60}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295422} {:answer/id #uuid "fe70b77f-b705-56cc-958e-a95fdd4b7591" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{30}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295423}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b1b7a55-6483-504b-8ec1-fd46abab4a14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{45}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295420} :answer-field/id #uuid "558ceb14-ef43-5e3d-a945-6c223e296a88" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295418}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9a7d0a24-b1bb-5537-9873-f6cb09e77afd" :question/math-academy-id "q-82178" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-{90}^{∘}\\le \\theta \\le {90}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's take a look at the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14035417/assets/d93c490adbb5cdbc0e9a09b60eea9b82ab93be8e871e334423abc17ddd62955e.png)\n\nFrom the values on the unit circle, we see that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡({315}^{∘})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nHowever, ${315}^{∘}$ does not lie within the range of $\\theta .$ To get an angle that's coterminal with ${315}^{∘}$ that lies within the range of $\\theta ,$ we subtract ${360}^{∘}.$\n\n$$\n{315}^{∘}-{360}^{∘}=-{45}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=-{45}^{∘}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295424 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e89b620-641a-5dbf-85f6-ca0f7a2fad80" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Arcsine or Arccosine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b4a99a2-5ac1-5865-b7dd-7a97879dd500" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295426} {:answer/id #uuid "be820f1c-309e-574f-a9c3-3a625db1d0c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295427} {:answer/id #uuid "4dbbbc76-a58e-5735-8f3b-a60d8f2e3e99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295428} {:answer/id #uuid "09ea5dac-abb1-58fd-b779-4abc4639b0ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295429} {:answer/id #uuid "34eccf4e-55cf-5f06-8e8c-d62fc63bc887" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295430}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be820f1c-309e-574f-a9c3-3a625db1d0c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295427} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9debef3-bda0-5742-bfb7-bae8a73b245f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295425}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "03d6506f-d3e5-5cb8-9458-da47d37af704" :question/math-academy-id "q-9091" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{1}{2})=$" :question/worked-solution "The range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi .\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta $ spans the first and second quadrants.\n\nSince $-\\frac{1}{2}$ is negative, our answer is an angle that lies in the second quadrant, where cosine is negative.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14035417/assets/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the values in the second quadrant, we see that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\n$$"}]}
